::ENG4_U03_L01_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.R.05] Passage: A report about food waste begins with the scale of the problem, explains causes such as overbuying and unclear labels, and ends with household and grocery-store solutions. Which structure is most evident? {
=A. Problem-cause-solution#Correct. The report moves from problem to causes to solutions.
~B. Fictional plot#The passage is informational.
~C. Random list#The order is purposeful.
~D. Personal diary#The passage is not personal reflection.
}
::ENG4_U03_L01_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.R.05] Passage: A report about food waste includes percentages for restaurants, grocery stores, and households but no visual. Which text feature would most improve reader understanding? {
~A. A decorative border#Decoration does not clarify data.
~B. A fictional dialogue#Dialogue would not clarify the data.
=C. A chart comparing waste by source#Correct. A chart would help readers compare categories.
~D. A glossary of unrelated terms#Unrelated terms would distract.
}
::ENG4_U03_L01_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Food labels can confuse buyers when "sell by" dates are mistaken for safety deadlines. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. Food labels are never confusing.#This contradicts the passage.
=B. Buyers may misunderstand sell-by dates as safety warnings.#Correct. This preserves the meaning.
~C. Sell-by dates always mean food is unsafe.#This is the confusion the passage describes.
~D. Buyers never read food labels.#This is not stated.
}
::ENG4_U03_L01_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: The report recommends practical interventions that stores can adopt quickly. What does interventions mean here? {
~A. Unrelated opinions#This is not the meaning.
~B. Decorative labels#This is too narrow and unrelated.
~C. Historical events#This is unrelated.
=D. Actions designed to address a problem#Correct
}
::ENG4_U03_L01_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: A writer revises a report sentence. Which sentence is correct? {
=A. The chart compares household, restaurant, and grocery-store waste.#Correct
~B. The chart compare household, restaurant, and grocery-store waste.#The verb should be compares.
~C. The chart compares household restaurant and grocery-store waste.#A comma is needed after household.
~D. the chart compares household, restaurant, and grocery-store waste.#The sentence should begin with a capital letter.
}
::ENG4_U03_L01_Q06::[MLA.ENG4.R.05] Passage: A technology article defines artificial intelligence in paragraph one, gives three workplace examples in paragraphs two through four, and ends by explaining ethical concerns. What would make the structure more effective? {
~A. Removing the definition before examples#Readers need the definition first.
~B. Replacing all examples with jokes#That would weaken the article.
=C. Adding headings such as "Definition," "Examples," and "Concerns"#Correct
~D. Moving ethical concerns before the topic is introduced#That would likely confuse readers.
}
::ENG4_U03_L01_Q07::[MLA.ENG4.R.05] Passage: A technology article defines artificial intelligence, gives workplace examples, and explains ethical concerns. Why is the current order mostly effective? {
~A. It hides the topic until the end.#The topic is introduced at the beginning.
=B. It begins with background before moving to examples and concerns.#Correct
~C. It avoids all explanation.#The passage includes definition and examples.
~D. It uses only emotional appeals.#The description is informational.
}
::ENG4_U03_L01_Q08::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Ethical concerns include privacy, accuracy, and fairness when automated systems make decisions about people. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. Automated systems solve every ethical concern.#This contradicts the passage.
~B. Privacy is the only concern.#The passage names three concerns.
~C. Ethical concerns are unrelated to people.#The passage says decisions are about people.
=D. Automated decisions raise questions about privacy, accuracy, and fairness.#Correct
}
::ENG4_U03_L01_Q09::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: The article's organization is coherent because each section builds on the previous one. What does coherent mean? {
=A. Clearly connected and understandable#Correct
~B. Completely random#This is the opposite.
~C. Visually decorative#This is not the meaning.
~D. Impossible to revise#This is unrelated.
}
::ENG4_U03_L01_Q10::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: Choose the clearest sentence. {
~A. Because the definition appears first readers can understand, the later examples.#The comma placement is incorrect.
~B. Because the definition appearing first, readers understand the later examples.#The opening clause is awkward.
=C. Because the definition appears first, readers can understand the later examples.#Correct
~D. because the definition appears first, readers can understand the later examples.#The sentence should begin with a capital letter.
}
::ENG4_U03_L01_Q11::[MLA.ENG4.R.05] Passage: A health article gives five statistics in a row before explaining what any of them mean. What is the main weakness? {
~A. It has no information at all.#It has statistics.
=B. It may overwhelm readers because data appears before explanation.#Correct
~C. It uses too many definitions before data.#The problem is the opposite.
~D. It is a fictional narrative.#It is a health article.
}
::ENG4_U03_L01_Q12::[MLA.ENG4.R.05] Passage: A health article gives five statistics in a row before explaining what any of them mean. Which revision would improve effectiveness? {
~A. Remove every explanation from the article.#That would make the problem worse.
~B. Add unrelated celebrity quotes.#Unrelated quotes would distract.
~C. Put the conclusion before the topic.#That would likely confuse readers.
=D. Add brief explanations after each key statistic.#Correct
}
::ENG4_U03_L01_Q13::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Data without explanation can leave readers uncertain about which numbers matter most. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
=A. Readers may need explanation to understand the importance of data.#Correct
~B. Readers never need data.#This is not stated.
~C. All numbers matter equally.#The passage says readers may not know which matter most.
~D. Explanation makes data impossible to understand.#This reverses the meaning.
}
::ENG4_U03_L01_Q14::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: The article needs context for the statistics. What does context mean here? {
~A. A spelling error#This is unrelated.
~B. A random decoration#This is not context.
=C. Surrounding information that helps explain meaning#Correct
~D. A conclusion with no support#This is not the meaning.
}
::ENG4_U03_L01_Q15::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: Which sentence uses standard conventions? {
~A. Statistics are useful but, readers need explanation.#The comma is misplaced.
=B. Statistics are useful, but readers need explanation.#Correct
~C. Statistics is useful, but readers need explanation.#The subject and verb do not agree.
~D. statistics are useful, but readers need explanation.#The sentence should begin with a capital letter.
}
::ENG4_U03_L01_Q16::[MLA.ENG4.R.05] Passage: A policy brief uses a heading labeled "Background" before explaining why a city lacks affordable housing. What is the function of the heading? {
~A. It proves the policy is wrong.#A heading alone does not prove that.
~B. It replaces the need for evidence.#Headings organize evidence but do not replace it.
~C. It makes the topic fictional.#The brief is informational.
=D. It signals that the section gives context needed to understand the problem.#Correct
}
::ENG4_U03_L01_Q17::[MLA.ENG4.R.05] Passage: A policy brief has headings for "Background," "Current Barriers," and "Possible Solutions." Why is this structure effective? {
=A. It helps readers move from context to problem details to recommendations.#Correct
~B. It hides the recommendations.#The recommendations are labeled.
~C. It avoids organization.#The headings show organization.
~D. It removes the central topic.#The headings support the topic.
}
::ENG4_U03_L01_Q18::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Current barriers include zoning limits, high construction costs, and limited public transportation near available land. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. Transportation is the only barrier.#The passage names three barriers.
~B. There are no zoning limits.#The passage says zoning limits are a barrier.
=C. The brief names several barriers to affordable housing development.#Correct
~D. Construction costs are low.#The passage says they are high.
}
::ENG4_U03_L01_Q19::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: The brief describes several barriers to affordable housing. What does barriers mean here? {
~A. Celebrations#This is unrelated.
=B. Obstacles or problems that make progress difficult#Correct
~C. Decorative signs#This is too literal and not contextual.
~D. Conclusions#This is not the meaning.
}
::ENG4_U03_L01_Q20::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: Which sentence is punctuated correctly? {
~A. The brief explains zoning limits high costs and transportation barriers.#Commas are missing.
~B. The brief, explains zoning limits, high costs, and transportation barriers.#The comma after brief is incorrect.
~C. The brief explains, zoning limits high costs, and transportation barriers.#The comma after explains is incorrect.
=D. The brief explains zoning limits, high costs, and transportation barriers.#Correct
}
::ENG4_U03_L01_Q21::[MLA.ENG4.R.05] Passage: A climate article begins with a personal story, then shifts to national data, then explains local policy options. Which evaluation is strongest? {
=A. The structure can be effective if the personal story introduces the human stakes before broader evidence.#Correct
~B. The structure is bad because all personal stories are useless.#This is too absolute.
~C. The structure has no organization.#The sequence is clear.
~D. The structure is fiction only.#The article includes data and policy.
}
::ENG4_U03_L01_Q22::[MLA.ENG4.R.05] Passage: A climate article begins with a personal story, then shifts to national data, then explains local policy options. What transition would best support coherence? {
~A. "Nothing in this story connects to data."#This breaks coherence.
~B. "The article will now discuss an unrelated recipe."#This is irrelevant.
=C. "This single neighborhood experience reflects a broader national pattern."#Correct
~D. "Stories and data can never appear together."#This is false.
}
::ENG4_U03_L01_Q23::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: A single neighborhood experience can reflect a broader national pattern. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. Local examples can never matter.#This contradicts the passage.
=B. One local example may illustrate a larger trend.#Correct
~C. National patterns are always unrelated to neighborhoods.#This contradicts the passage.
~D. The passage is only about one recipe.#This is unrelated.
}
::ENG4_U03_L01_Q24::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: The transition supports coherence between the personal story and the national data. What does transition mean here? {
~A. A type of statistic#This is not the meaning.
~B. A random image#This is unrelated.
~C. A spelling rule#This is unrelated.
=D. A word or phrase that connects ideas#Correct
}
::ENG4_U03_L01_Q25::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: Which sentence is clearest? {
=A. The transition connects the story to the data, so readers understand why both sections matter.#Correct
~B. The transition, connects the story to the data so readers understand why both sections matter.#The comma after transition is incorrect.
~C. The transition connect the story to the data, so readers understand why both sections matter.#The verb should be connects.
~D. the transition connects the story to the data, so readers understand why both sections matter.#The sentence should begin with a capital letter.
}